From 2d04301d0c7fa0bbff234a64f283472efe6adf90 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mahesh Vaidya <mahesh.vaidya@altera.com>
Date: Thu, 2 Jul 2026 22:04:54 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] net: stmmac: fix TX queue timeout caused by disabling NAPI
 during tc offload

stmmac_setup_tc_block_cb() disables and re-enables NAPI on all queues
around every classifier offload operation, via
__stmmac_disable_all_queues() and stmmac_enable_all_queues(). This opens
a window during which a TX completion interrupt can be serviced while NAPI
is disabled.

napi_disable() leaves NAPIF_STATE_SCHED set on the NAPI instance. While it
is set, napi_schedule_prep() returns false, so when a TX completion
interrupt arrives in this window, stmmac_napi_check() cannot schedule the
poll: the DMA interrupt status is read and acknowledged in hardware, but
__napi_schedule() is never called. napi_enable() later clears
NAPIF_STATE_SCHED but does not schedule a poll. Because the interrupt was
already acknowledged, no further interrupt is raised for those descriptors,
so stmmac_tx_clean() never runs for the affected queue.

The frames themselves are transmitted successfully; only their completion
is never processed. dirty_tx stays behind cur_tx and, crucially, the
completed bytes are never reported to BQL via netdev_tx_completed_queue().
The queue's BQL outstanding byte count therefore keeps growing until it
exceeds the limit, at which point the stack sets __QUEUE_STATE_STACK_XOFF
and freezes the queue.

Normally BQL clears STACK_XOFF once completions are reported, but here
that never happens, so the freeze is permanent: no further packets are
enqueued, the queue's trans_start stops advancing, and after watchdog_timeo
the netdev TX watchdog fires and resets the adapter. The queue is never
stopped by the driver (netif_tx_queue_stopped() reads false); it is frozen
only by BQL. This was confirmed at the timeout with drv_xoff=0,
stack_xoff=1 and BQL outstanding (e.g. 310 bytes) above the
limit (e.g. 200 bytes).

The problem is easily reproduced by repeatedly adding and removing tc
flower filters (for example VLAN-priority to traffic-class steering) while
background traffic flows.

Fix this by rescheduling NAPI on all queues after re-enabling them in
stmmac_setup_tc_block_cb(). Any completion whose napi_schedule() was
dropped while NAPI was disabled is then processed: stmmac_tx_clean()
reclaims the pending descriptors and reports the completed bytes to
BQL, which clears STACK_XOFF and lets the queue resume. Both the tx_napi
and rxtx_napi (XDP zero-copy) paths are handled.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Fixes: 4dbbe8dde848 ("net: stmmac: Add support for U32 TC filter using Flexible RX Parser")
Signed-off-by: Mahesh Vaidya <mahesh.vaidya@altera.com>
---
 .../net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/stmmac_main.c | 27 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 27 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/stmmac_main.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/stmmac_main.c
index 65f6853c1b69..d71253a0e28f 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/stmmac_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/stmmac_main.c
@@ -6222,6 +6222,7 @@ static int stmmac_setup_tc_block_cb(enum tc_setup_type type, void *type_data,
 {
 	struct stmmac_priv *priv = cb_priv;
 	int ret = -EOPNOTSUPP;
+	u32 q, maxq;
 
 	if (!tc_cls_can_offload_and_chain0(priv->dev, type_data))
 		return ret;
@@ -6240,6 +6241,32 @@ static int stmmac_setup_tc_block_cb(enum tc_setup_type type, void *type_data,
 	}
 
 	stmmac_enable_all_queues(priv);
+
+	/* napi_disable() above leaves NAPIF_STATE_SCHED set, so a TX/RX
+	 * completion IRQ arriving during the disabled window has its
+	 * napi_schedule() dropped while the IRQ is still acknowledged in
+	 * HW. napi_enable() does not reschedule, so the completion is
+	 * never reaped: the bytes are never returned to BQL, the queue is
+	 * frozen by STACK_XOFF, and the TX watchdog resets the adapter.
+	 * Kick every NAPI so any dropped completion is processed.
+	 */
+	maxq = max(priv->plat->rx_queues_to_use,
+		   priv->plat->tx_queues_to_use);
+
+	for (q = 0; q < maxq; q++) {
+		struct stmmac_channel *ch = &priv->channel[q];
+
+		if (stmmac_xdp_is_enabled(priv) &&
+		    test_bit(q, priv->af_xdp_zc_qps)) {
+			napi_schedule(&ch->rxtx_napi);
+			continue;
+		}
+		if (q < priv->plat->rx_queues_to_use)
+			napi_schedule(&ch->rx_napi);
+		if (q < priv->plat->tx_queues_to_use)
+			napi_schedule(&ch->tx_napi);
+	}
+
 	return ret;
 }
 
